Maya (docs): Opening a docs PR to replace legacy curl examples in troubleshooting with streaming SDK snippets + a 'trim then fallback' pattern for context-limit and 429 scenarios. Draft branch: https://go/repo/drafts/trim-fallback-sdk :wave
Evan (eng-runtime): +1. Can we show a tiny table that contrasts Hosted vs Dedicated behavior for burst throttling and autoscale delay? People keep assuming identical semantics.
Priya (sdk): I can add Go + Python streaming examples. For 429s we recommend: 1) small exponential backoff, 2) try a trimmed prompt (drop nonessential history), 3) fall back to compact model. I'll include a helper trimHistory(messages, maxTokens) util.
Maya (docs): Example curl to migrate is the one on the current page — keeping it for legacy but adding a note to prefer streaming SDKs.
Jon (support): We have an anonymized customer trace that shows repeated 429s when the client retries without jitter. I'll paste a sanitized sequence to show the difference between naive retry vs jittered backoff + fallback.
Priya (sdk): Quick Go streaming sketch I'll add to PR: ```
client := redwood.NewClient(os.Getenv("REDWOOD_KEY"))
stream := client.Chat.Stream(ctx, redwood.ChatParams{Model: "redwood-1", Messages: msgs})
for chunk := range stream.Chan() {
  fmt.Print(chunk.Content) // handle partial tokens
}
if err := stream.Err(); err != nil {
  if errors.Is(err, redwood.ErrRateLimited) {
    // fallback: trim history and call compact model
    trimmed := trimHistory(msgs, 1024)
    resp, _ := client.Chat.Create(ctx, redwood.ChatParams{Model: "redwood-compact-1", Messages: trimmed})
    fmt.Println("[FALLBACK]", resp.Output)
  } else {
    log.Fatal(err)
  }
}
```
Evan (eng-runtime): Love the practical code. Also add a one-paragraph note: because Dedicated pools may have cold-start autoscale lag, trimming + model fallback can have better p99 latency than waiting for burst capacity to warm.
